{
  "description": "Conformance corpus for the sandbox `console`. Every console method takes any value and never throws, and the host's console handler receives each argument formatted the same way in every runtime: as data in a JavaScript runtime, whose handler receives the arguments themselves, and as text in a runtime whose handler receives one message string. The expected output was recorded from `@xriptjs/runtime`.",
  "notes": [
    "Each case's `script` runs in a fresh runtime built from `manifest`, with no bindings and a console handler that records every call: its severity, and its arguments (a JavaScript runtime) or its message (a runtime whose handler receives a string). Every script ends by evaluating `sentinel`, and executing it must return `sentinel`: a console call never throws, whatever it is given, so the script always runs on.",
    "`logs` lists the console calls in the order they were made. `severity` is the handler's severity: `console.log` and `console.info` are `info`, and `warn`, `error`, `debug`, and `trace` are their own names.",
    "`args` is what a JavaScript runtime's handler receives, spelled with the markers of `value-fidelity-tests.json`. A runner encodes what its handler received the same way and compares at every depth, key order included. A host object that is not a plain object or an array (a `Map`, a `Date`, an `Error` instance, a function) is a mismatch: the handler receives data.",
    "How an argument becomes data. A string, number, BigInt, boolean, `null`, or `undefined` is itself, `-0` and `NaN` included. A symbol is a fresh host symbol with the same description. A function is its source text, `String(fn)`. An object follows `JSON.stringify`'s rules (own enumerable string keys in property order, `toJSON` applied and skipped when it throws, `Number`/`String`/`Boolean`/`BigInt` wrappers unwrapped, symbols dropped from objects and `null` in arrays, functions omitted from objects and `null` in arrays), except that it keeps what JSON cannot spell (`undefined`, `NaN`, the infinities, `-0`, BigInt) and keeps a cycle as a reference to the ancestor's copy. So a `Map` or `Set` is `{}` and a `Date` is its ISO string. A top-level object that has a `name`, `message`, or `stack` other than as an own enumerable key (inherited, or own and non-enumerable, as on an `Error`, whatever its prototype) gets each of them copied onto its data form, after its own keys and in that order; when the object has a `toJSON` method, they are copied onto the object `toJSON` returned. A copied property that throws when read, or holds `undefined`, a symbol, or a function, is left out, and the argument stays readable. Every getter runs once: whether an object carries `name`, `message`, `stack`, or `toJSON` is decided without running their getters.",
    "An argument whose reading throws (a revoked Proxy at any depth, a getter that throws, a function whose `toString` throws, a value nested deeper than the runtime carries) reaches the handler as the string `[unreadable: <description>]`, and the call's other arguments are unaffected. The description of a thrown object with a non-empty string `message` is `name: message` (`Error` standing in for a `name` that is not a string), the bare name when the message is empty, and of anything else `String(thrown)`. An execution-limit stop (a timeout or a cancellation) while reading is not an unreadable argument: it still ends the script.",
    "`message` is what a runtime whose handler receives one string receives: each argument rendered as text, joined by single spaces. A string is itself. An `Error` (an argument for which `instanceof Error` holds in the sandbox) is rendered from its data form's `name` and `message`: `name: message` when the message is a non-empty string, the bare name otherwise, and `Error` standing in for a name that is not a string or was left out. Its other properties and its stack are not part of the text. Any other object that carries `name` and `message`, copied or its own, renders as data like every other object. Every other argument is the text of its data form above: `undefined`, `null`, `true`, `false`; a number as JavaScript spells it (`String(n)`) except `-0`, which is `-0`, and `NaN`, `Infinity`, `-Infinity`; a BigInt as its digits followed by `n`; a symbol as `Symbol(description)` or `Symbol()`; an array as `[a,b]` and an object as `{\"key\":value}`, with no spaces, keys and nested strings quoted as `JSON.stringify` quotes them, and a reference back to an ancestor as `[Circular]`. A function's data form and an unreadable argument's are strings, so they render as themselves.",
    "Where text is the engine's own (an error's `stack`, the message of an error the engine threw, the engine's description of a revoked Proxy), the expected value is a matcher: `{ \"$expect\": \"string\" }` matches any string, and with `prefix` or `suffix` the string must also start or end with them. A matcher with `\"optional\": true` stands for a property that may also be absent: an engine that formats an error's `stack` lazily from its `name` and `message` has no stack to give when reading those throws or yields a symbol. A matcher may stand for an argument, for a property of one, or for a whole `message`. It has no `$xript` key, so it is never a value marker."
  ],
